  "answer_text": "When someone has a \"lazy eye\"\u2014which, in social interactions, usually refers to **strabismus** (misaligned eyes, where one eye points in a different direction than the other)\u2014it can sometimes make establishing eye contact feel confusing or awkward. \n\nPeople with strabismus are often highly aware of their condition and can feel self-conscious during conversations. \n\n### 1. Look at the \"Dominant\" Eye\nIn almost all cases of strabismus, one eye is aligned and actively focusing on you (the dominant eye), while the other eye is turned. \n* **The Technique:** Focus your gaze on the eye that is looking directly at you. This is the eye they are using to see you, so looking at it will create a genuine sense of eye contact for both of you. \n* **Why it works:** It feels natural, acknowledges their active gaze, and keeps you from getting distracted by the misaligned eye.\n\n### 2. Look at the Bridge of the Nose (The Classic Trick)\nIf you find it difficult to focus on just one eye, or if you feel your gaze starting to drift, try this classic interpersonal trick.\n* **The Technique:** Focus your eyes on the **bridge of their nose**, right between their eyes. \n* **Why it works:** To the other person, it looks exactly like you are making direct, steady eye contact. For you, it provides a neutral, non-moving focal point that prevents your eyes from wandering.\n\n### 3. What to Avoid\n* **Avoid \"eye-darting\":** Do not let your gaze bounce back and forth between their two eyes. People with strabismus can easily spot this \"ping-pong\" gaze, which signals to them that you are focused on their misalignment, often making them feel uncomfortable or self-conscious.\n* **Avoid staring at the turned eye:** If you focus on the eye that is turned away, they will know you aren't actually making eye contact with them, which can make the interaction feel clinical or awkward.\n\n### 4. Practice Natural Eye Contact Flow\nRemember that \"good\" eye contact doesn't mean locking eyes uninterrupted for the entire conversation. \n* It is entirely normal to look away occasionally, especially when *you* are the one speaking or thinking. \n* Try to maintain steadier contact when *they* are speaking to show you are listening. \n\n### 5. Shift Your Mindset\nUltimately, the most important thing is to **focus on the conversation, not the eyes**. People with strabismus want to be treated just like anyone else. Once you get past the initial few seconds of adjusting your gaze, focus entirely on what they are saying. Your brain will naturally adapt, the physical misalignment will fade into the background, and you will connect on a human level.",
